---

Bluetooth management using bluetoothctl (from bluez) on Artix Linux
with OpenRC.

@code bash
# Install bluetooth stack
sudo pacman -S bluez bluez-utils

# Start bluetooth service (OpenRC)
sudo rc-service bluetoothd start
sudo rc-update add bluetoothd default    # enable at boot

# Check adapter status
bluetoothctl show                # adapter info
bluetoothctl list                # list adapters (if multiple)

@text
rfkill manages wireless device soft/hard blocks. A soft-blocked
adapter cannot be used until unblocked.

@code bash
# List wireless devices
rfkill list
rfkill list bluetooth

# Unblock bluetooth
sudo rfkill unblock bluetooth

# Block bluetooth (disable radio)
sudo rfkill block bluetooth

# Check if blocked
rfkill | grep bluetooth

@text
Interactive pairing with bluetoothctl. The tool provides an interactive
shell but commands can also be passed directly.

@code bash
# Enter interactive mode
bluetoothctl

# --- Inside bluetoothctl shell ---

# Power on the adapter
power on

# Start scanning for devices
scan on
# Wait for your device to appear...
# [NEW] Device AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF My Headphones

# Stop scanning
scan off

# Pair with a device
pair AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
# Confirm PIN if prompted

# Trust device (auto-connect in future)
trust AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

# Connect
connect AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

# Disconnect
disconnect AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

# Remove a paired device
remove AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

# List known devices
devices
devices Paired
devices Bonded

# Exit
exit

@text
Non-interactive one-line commands for scripting.

@code bash
# Power on
bluetoothctl power on

# Scan, pair, trust, connect in sequence
bluetoothctl scan on &
sleep 10
bluetoothctl scan off
bluetoothctl pair AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
bluetoothctl trust AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
bluetoothctl connect AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

# Check connection status
bluetoothctl info AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

# List paired devices
bluetoothctl devices Paired

@text
Troubleshooting common bluetooth issues.

@code bash
# Check if bluetooth service is running
rc-service bluetoothd status

# Restart bluetooth
sudo rc-service bluetoothd restart

# Check kernel module
lsmod | grep btusb
sudo modprobe btusb              # load if missing

# Debug logs
sudo btmon                       # live bluetooth monitor
journalctl -b | grep -i bluetooth   # if using a logger

# Remove and re-pair a problematic device
bluetoothctl remove AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF
bluetoothctl scan on
# Then pair/trust/connect again

# Bluetooth configuration
# /etc/bluetooth/main.conf
#   [General]
#   AutoEnable=true              # power on adapter at boot
#   FastConnectable=true
#   [Policy]
#   AutoEnable=true
